         <h3 id="conventions">Conventions, in brief</h3>
         <ul>
-          <li><strong>Balance through a date.</strong> For each account, the sum of debit minus credit over the entries dated on or before that date. Assets other than accumulated depreciation have a normal debit balance, and so do dividends and expenses. The other classes, accumulated depreciation included, have credit balances, and the statements show those credit balances as positive amounts.</li>
+          <li><strong>Balance through a date.</strong> For each account, the sum of debit minus credit over the entries dated on or before that date. Assets other than accumulated depreciation have a normal debit balance, and so do dividends and expenses. Liabilities, common stock, revenue and accumulated depreciation have credit balances, and the statements show those credit balances as positive amounts.</li>
           <li><strong>The month.</strong> The period that ends on <code>period_end</code> is the calendar month that ends that day. Its previous month end is the last day of the month before. The flow of an account over the month is its balance through <code>period_end</code> less its balance through the previous month end.</li>
           <li><strong>Balance sheet.</strong> Assets are the debit balances, with accumulated depreciation shown as a positive amount and subtracted to give <code>net_equipment</code>. Liabilities and common stock are shown as positive amounts. Retained earnings are the net income to date less the dividends to date, where net income to date is revenue less cost of goods sold, operating expenses and depreciation.</li>
           <li><strong>Income statement.</strong> Sales are the negative of the sales flow. Cost, expense and depreciation flows are shown as positive amounts. Gross profit is sales less cost of goods sold, and net income is gross profit less operating expenses and depreciation.</li>
